{
  "id": "binary-tree-right-side-view",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-right-side-view",
  "slug": "binary-tree-right-side-view",
  "title": "Binary Tree Right Side View",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you only need one value per level - which position in a left-to-right level order traversal is that?",
    "process the queue one full level at a time, the same way you would for a normal level order traversal",
    "as you drain a level's nodes left to right, just keep overwriting a 'last value seen this level' variable",
    "after finishing each level's batch, that last-seen value is exactly the one visible from the right - append it to the result"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "enqueuing children in left-then-right order but reading the FIRST node of each level instead of the last",
    "trying a DFS-right-first approach but forgetting to only record a value the first time a new depth is reached, which then gets overwritten incorrectly by a later left-subtree visit at the same depth",
    "skipping levels that only contain a lone left-child node, assuming the rightmost visible node must have come from a right-child position"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node here\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Imagine standing to the right of the tree and looking straight at it: for every level, only the rightmost node is visible. Return the values you would see, ordered from the top level down to the bottom level.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= tree.length <= 2000",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node value",
      "tree[i] == -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "a tree that is entirely a left-leaning chain, where the only node per level happens to be the left child",
      "a level whose deepest-going node comes from a left subtree while a shallower right subtree has already ended"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[3, 20, 7]",
        "expectedOutput": "[3, 20, 7]",
        "explanation": "level 0's only node is 3; level 1's rightmost node is 20; level 2's rightmost node is 7"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[1, 3, 7]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 3, 7]",
        "explanation": "the rightmost value at each level of a perfectly complete tree is always its rightmost child"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, -101, 4]",
        "output": "[1, 3, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 3, 4]",
        "explanation": "level 2's only real node is 4 (node 2's right child), so it is the rightmost, and only, node visible there"
      }
    ],
